	     016: Lost Grrrl Found         Lost Grrrl Found is creating a whole new art form with their musical/punk show mashup. The show’s director and co-writers talked with Brooklyn Buzz to celebrate their upcoming EP and the fact that you can see their project performed LIVE this Saturday (10/24/2026) at Stone Circle Theatre. In reading (or listening to) this interview, discover how the riot grrrl movement is kept alive and fighting today, how lesbian punk bands are the perfect avenue for exploring complicated friendship and queer community, and how to attend this one-of-a-kind performance. Lost Grrrl Found’s EP, Pupal Phase, will be released October 23rd and tickets for their Stone Circle Theatre performance on October 24th can be found  HERE . This is something you don’t want to miss! 







   Continue reading     






	     015: Pet Sounds Music Festival III         Jake Murray returns to Brooklyn Buzz to talk about the BIGGER and BETTER Pet Sounds Music Festival coming up this September! Now TWO jam-packed nights with 16 bands total, real-life fighting gladiators, and fabulous drag queen performances, this is a DIY festival you won’t want to miss. Listen to the audio included in the article or read the interview here to have Jake tell you all about how the festival has evolved, the community that has been built within the festival, and even some Brooklyn Buzz exclusive information about the bands and partnerships that are part of the weekend! Make sure to stop by Rubulad on September 18th and 19th where you can listen to great music, meet new people in the community, or even just chill with a hotdog in hand. 







   Continue reading     





	     014: Um, Jennifer?         Fig Regan and Eli Scarpati discuss the newest and hottest music in the trans-slut rock genre from their band Um, Jennifer? After rolling out a few spectacular singles, their eagerly-awaited EP, Dirty Twice, is now available for all ears to enjoy. Not only do they talk about the album’s portrayal of a full relationship from beginning to end, but they also discuss how liberating it is to mess with your gender, how to have fun promoting the work you’re proud of, and how to live confidently by controlling your own self perception. If you’re interested in the NYC trans and queer music scene, this is the band and interview for you.  
“Girl we are in the water, let’s go swimming.”  







   Continue reading     





	     013: CRAB         Crab is a New York band fueled by evolution, experimentation, and a firm belief that Magic Is Real. In this interview, Chris, Chuck, and Kasey take us inside the band’s DIY beginnings, their increasingly exhilarating live performances, and the NYC creative community that continues to shape their sound and identity. After unpacking their newest singles (which include Hair / Zephyr, Unlimited, and Twinkle / Shine), they share what’s ahead for Crab as they push the boundaries of music, art, and magic!  







   Continue reading     













	    012: Diner Pets         

Brooklyn’s DIY scene thrives on community, and  Jake Murray  of  Diner Pets  has built a project rooted in collaboration, friendship, and a love for music. In this interview, we talk about the origins of  Diner Pets , their emotionally charged new single, and the evolution of the  Pet Sounds Music Banquet , a chaotically creative festival that’s quickly becoming a staple of the local scene. Jake also shares what it takes to organize a large-scale DIY event and why spaces like these matter more than ever right now. The upcoming  Pet Sounds Music Banquet  will be taking place on April 11th at Rubulad!









   Continue reading    












	     011: Best Actress         NYC’s Best Actress started in secret, born from three musicians who met on local bills (including members of Our Lady of Sorrows and No Big). Now, they step into their debut era together with   I Know It Sounds Bad  , a tender, self-aware full-length album born from discovery and careful tinkering. The project began “intentionally and quietly” before blossoming into a 28-minute attic-pop dream. In our conversation, the trio opens up about embracing sincerity over irony, working with producer  Nate Mendelsohn  of Market, and why the album title is both a joke and a manifesto.  







   Continue reading     








	    010: Ballpark Figure 

 We interviewed Pratt Radio alumna Basia Kurlender about how radio has shaped where she is now. Her company, Ballpark Figure, provides a platform for emerging artists to perform in Long Island communities that lack access to live music and entertainment. 







   Continue reading     






	     009: The Baxbys   

    The Baxbys are an indie band from Connecticut who found their sound while hanging out in Amherst. We chatted with Alex, James, and Chris (Spencer was enjoying some downtime at Washington Square Park) before their show at Mercury Lounge. Their new single,“In a Cage,” is part of an upcoming album inspired by art, and everyday life. We also got to chat about pasta and flea market fun. Their music is honest, easy to connect with, and pretty much your next favorite indie boy band.  

   Continue reading     




	     008: Sarah Michelle Lee   

    Back in the fall, Brooklyn Buzz interviewed Brooklyn-based singer and songwriter Sarah Michelle Lee, who recently launched her solo project. Sarah shares her inspirations, the journey behind her music and music videos, and the influence that NYC’s vibrant music scene has had on her. With a focus on love and vulnerability, her songs evoke a unique, cozy atmosphere perfect for those reflective moments. As Sarah teases upcoming projects and releases, she gives us a glimpse into the exciting future ahead for both her solo work and her band monarch.'s next steps.     

   Continue reading     





	     007: Mother Feather   

    Brooklyn Buzz interviewed Ann Courtney, the frontwoman of Mother Feather, to celebrate the release of their newest album, “KICK3R.” Mother Feather is a high-energy rock band that effortlessly blends glam and punk with a vibrant, theatrical flare. This interview was originally recorded and streamed on WPIR in November -- the link to that audio can be found below this paragraph if you want to hear how much we laughed and enjoyed ourselves throughout. Ann had such a fantastic and friendly energy as we discussed the personal meanings behind their new songs, what it has been like performing live in a post-Covid world, and how to be your most authentic self while celebrating the people in your life.  

   Continue reading     











	     006: The Roulettes     
 In early November, Brooklyn Buzz interviewed The Roulettes — a lively, riot grrl-inspired band based in NYC — as a celebration of the release of their newest EP, “Mommy Comes Back.” Talking with Rebecca, Rachel, and Alice was a blast. They discuss the deep meanings behind their latest songs and their reunion after a hiatus of releasing music, while also mentioning Bill Murray, working as a teacher, recent Halloween costumes, and much more!  

   Continue reading     










	    005: The Cyrkle Revival   

 Our board members Eve Mikkelson and Arzu Oran interviews Pat McLoughlin and Don Dannemann, two of the members of the iconic 1960s rock band The Cyrkle.

     Continue reading      













	    004: RAEGAN   

 Nur Güzeldere interviews and photographs rising singer-songwriter RAEGAN. 

     Continue reading      







	    003: Lucca Dohr   

 Earlier this month on October 6th, Lucca Dohr heled an album release concert at Heaven Can Wait in New York City, featuring performances by fellow artist Graham Riley and Lauren Juzang, in celebration of his new EP June Bug which came out just the day before. Here, after catching the final one and a half songs of his live performance, I interrogated Lucca about music, film, youth, love, and Jeff Buckley. 

     Continue reading      








  002: DEBBY FRIDAY   

 I play DEBBY FRIDAY on the aux when I want to induct someone into a cult--the cult of FRIDAY. No matter the track, whether it's her early computerized BITCHPUNK days or her melodic hazey singles, I always get the same reaction from those who earwitness. Who is this?? they ask me, opening Shazam. The new single “Let u in” is something different from what I know of DEBBY FRIDAY.   
     Continue reading      






	    001: Lowertown   

   Last week we interviewed Lowertown, a sizzling, independent New York indie band—in anticipation of their new EP Skin Of My Teeth, dropping October 27th. In conversation with Olivia Osby and Avsha Weinberg, we discussed their upcoming European tour, the nature of live performance, social identity in both the physical and digital world, living in New York, and musical influences. As the band prepares to travel overseas, they open up about the new confidence they’ve found as musicians.  
       Continue reading     




